To implement the full role-based architecture and automatic synchronization you've described, you need to perform two sets of actions: 

1.  **Database Configuration**: You must run the SQL commands provided below in your Supabase SQL Editor. This sets up the automatic "Sync" between Supabase Auth and your `profiles` table.
2.  **App Updates**: I have updated the components to ensure they pass the correct "Identity Metadata" during user creation so the database knows exactly which role and organization to assign.

### 1. Database Schema & RLS (Run this in Supabase SQL Editor)

Copy and run this entire block in your Supabase project's SQL Editor:

```sql
-- 1. PROFILES TABLE (linked to auth.users)
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS public.profiles (
  id UUID REFERENCES auth.users ON DELETE CASCADE PRIMARY KEY,
  email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  name TEXT,
  role TEXT DEFAULT 'Volunteer', -- 'MasterAdmin', 'Organisation', 'Volunteer'
  organisation_id UUID REFERENCES public.organisations(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
  mobile TEXT,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

-- 2. AUTOMATIC SYNC FUNCTION
-- This reads the 'data' we send from the frontend and inserts it into the profiles table automatically.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user()
RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, email, name, role, organisation_id, mobile)
  VALUES (
    NEW.id,
    NEW.email,
    COALESCE(NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'name', 'User'),
    COALESCE(NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'role', 'Volunteer'),
    (NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'organisation_id')::uuid,
    NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'mobile'
  );
  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER;

-- 3. BIND THE TRIGGER
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS on_auth_user_created ON auth.users;
CREATE TRIGGER on_auth_user_created
  AFTER INSERT ON auth.users
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user();

-- 4. ROW LEVEL SECURITY (RLS) POLICIES
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE public.members ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- Master Admin: Can see/do everything
CREATE POLICY "Master Admin Full Access" ON public.profiles FOR ALL USING (
  (SELECT role FROM public.profiles WHERE id = auth.uid()) = 'MasterAdmin'
);

-- Org Admin: Can see volunteers in their org
CREATE POLICY "Org Admin Manage Own Volunteers" ON public.profiles FOR ALL USING (
  organisation_id = (SELECT organisation_id FROM public.profiles WHERE id = auth.uid())
);

-- Members Table RLS
CREATE POLICY "Master Admin Members Access" ON public.members FOR ALL USING (
  (SELECT role FROM public.profiles WHERE id = auth.uid()) = 'MasterAdmin'
);

CREATE POLICY "Org Admin Members Access" ON public.members FOR ALL USING (
  organisation_id = (SELECT organisation_id FROM public.profiles WHERE id = auth.uid())
);

CREATE POLICY "Volunteer Own Submissions" ON public.members FOR ALL USING (
  volunteer_id = auth.uid()
);
```

### 2. Application Logic Updates

The following changes ensure that the frontend sends the specific metadata required by the `handle_new_user` trigger.

