The "Access Denied" error is occurring because of a **deadlock** in your database's security rules (Row Level Security). Even though your login is correct, the database is getting stuck in an infinite loop trying to verify if you are allowed to see your own profile.

To fix this, you must run a **Repair Script** in your Supabase SQL Editor. This script creates a "bypass" function that breaks the loop and ensures your Master Admin account is correctly registered in the system.

### How to Fix "Access Denied" (3-Minute Fix)

1.  **Open Supabase**: Go to your [Supabase Dashboard](https://supabase.com/dashboard).
2.  **Go to SQL Editor**: Click the **SQL Editor** icon on the left sidebar (it looks like `>_`).
3.  **New Query**: Click **"+ New Query"**.
4.  **Paste and Run**: Paste the code below and click **RUN**.

```sql
-- 🚀 SSK SYSTEM REPAIR & RECURSION BUSTER
-- Run this once to fix "Access Denied" and "RLS_RECURSION_ERROR"

-- 1. Create a safe role-checker that bypasses the security loop
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.get_my_role()
RETURNS text AS $$
DECLARE
  r text;
BEGIN
  -- This bypasses RLS checks during the role lookup
  SELECT role INTO r FROM public.profiles WHERE id = auth.uid();
  RETURN COALESCE(r, 'Volunteer');
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER SET search_path = public;

-- 2. Clean up old, broken policies
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Admins have full access" ON public.profiles;
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Users can view own profile" ON public.profiles;
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Admins can manage organisations" ON public.organisations;
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Public view for organisations" ON public.organisations;

-- 3. Apply safe, non-recursive policies
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE public.organisations ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- Profiles: You can see yourself, Admins can see everyone
CREATE POLICY "Users can view own profile" ON public.profiles FOR SELECT USING (auth.uid() = id);
CREATE POLICY "Admins have full access" ON public.profiles FOR ALL USING (public.get_my_role() = 'MasterAdmin');

-- Organisations: Admins manage, everyone can view
CREATE POLICY "Admins can manage organisations" ON public.organisations FOR ALL USING (public.get_my_role() = 'MasterAdmin');
CREATE POLICY "Public view for organisations" ON public.organisations FOR SELECT USING (true);

-- 4. Force Create the Master Admin Profile
-- This ensures the system recognizes Masteradmin@ssk.com as the boss
INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, name, email, role, status)
SELECT id, 'Master Administrator', email, 'MasterAdmin', 'Active'
FROM auth.users 
WHERE LOWER(email) = 'masteradmin@ssk.com'
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET role = 'MasterAdmin', status = 'Active';
```

