To resolve the **`[object Object]`** error and the underlying **Trigger Failure**, we need to harden the error handling and deploy the **Final Integrity Fix**. 

The `[object Object]` occurs because the code is passing a raw error object into a string template (e.g., `addNotification("Error: " + error)`) instead of accessing `error.message`. More importantly, the trigger failure is blocking your database from syncing users to profiles.

### Step 1: Execute the Final Nuclear Fix
Go to your **Supabase SQL Editor**, paste this, and click **RUN**. This version uses an `EXCEPTION` block that makes it impossible for the database to block user signups, even if the sync fails.

```sql
-- 🔱 SSK "GOD-LEVEL" INTEGRITY REPAIR 🔱
-- This script force-heals the schema and makes the sync trigger un-crashable.

-- 1. Ensure Table Structure is Perfect
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS name text;
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS email text;
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS mobile text;
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS role text;
ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS status text DEFAULT 'Active';

-- Fix organisation_id type safely
DO $$ 
BEGIN 
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name='profiles' AND column_name='organisation_id' AND data_type='text') THEN
    ALTER TABLE public.profiles ALTER COLUMN organisation_id TYPE uuid USING organisation_id::uuid;
  ELSE
    ALTER TABLE public.profiles ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS organisation_id uuid;
  END IF;
END $$;

-- 2. Create the "Safe-Fail" Trigger Function
-- This version catches ALL errors and allows the signup to proceed.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user()
RETURNS trigger AS $$
DECLARE
  raw_org_id text;
  final_org_id uuid;
BEGIN
  BEGIN -- Inner block to catch errors
    raw_org_id := new.raw_user_meta_data->>'organisation_id';

    IF raw_org_id IS NOT NULL AND raw_org_id <> '' AND raw_org_id <> 'null' THEN
      BEGIN
        final_org_id := raw_org_id::uuid;
      EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN
        final_org_id := NULL;
      END;
    END IF;

    INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, name, email, role, organisation_id, mobile, status)
    VALUES (
      new.id,
      COALESCE(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'name', 'User Node'),
      new.email,
      COALESCE(new.raw_user_meta_data->>'role', 'Volunteer'),
      final_org_id,
      new.raw_user_meta_data->>'mobile',
      'Active'
    )
    ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
      role = EXCLUDED.role,
      organisation_id = EXCLUDED.organisation_id,
      name = EXCLUDED.name,
      mobile = EXCLUDED.mobile,
      status = EXCLUDED.status;

  EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN
    -- If sync fails, we LOG it but DO NOT stop the Auth signup.
    -- This allows the user to be created so the frontend can "Heal" it manually.
    RAISE WARNING 'Sync failed for user %, but Auth allowed to proceed.', new.id;
  END;

  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER;

-- 3. Reset the Trigger
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS on_auth_user_created ON auth.users;
CREATE TRIGGER on_auth_user_created
  AFTER INSERT ON auth.users
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user();

-- 4. Set Permissions
GRANT ALL ON public.profiles TO service_role;
GRANT ALL ON public.profiles TO postgres;
GRANT SELECT ON public.profiles TO authenticated, anon;
```

### Step 2: Application Fixes
I have updated `VolunteerDashboard.tsx`, `ManageOrganisations.tsx`, and `ManageVolunteers.tsx` to properly display error messages and prevent the `[object Object]` issue.

